It responds (negatively) to the whitespace that forms a block indent in code and
text, and to a few 4-character "fill" runs. The strength depends on the width of the
run, not on the surrounding text (layer 0 reads the token itself):

  * exactly 4 spaces (one indent level): strongest, about -1.5;
  * other multiples of 4 spaces (8, 12, 16, ... 32): about -0.8;
  * runs of 5 or 6 spaces: about -0.6;
  * a tab followed by 1 to 5 spaces (tab-indented line): about -0.6;
  * runs of 11, 15, 19, ... spaces (width = 3 mod 4): slightly positive, +0.25;
  * fill runs "...." / " ...." (dot leaders) and nbsp runs of 4: about -1.5 to -2;
  * everything else (words, punctuation, newlines, 1 to 3 spaces, bare tabs): 0.

The activation is set by the token alone, so the program looks only at the newest token.
